 "lines": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "The asteroid that killed the giant dinosaurs may have hit one of the unluckiest spots on Earth.",
   "from": 0,
   "to": 97
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Sixty-six million years ago, a rock six miles wide or more slams into what is now Mexico.",
   "from": 100,
   "to": 247
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "speaker": "dino",
   "text": "Why is the sky so bright?",
   "from": 251,
   "to": 311
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "It lands in a shallow sea, on rock packed with sulfur and buried oil.",
   "from": 315,
   "to": 413
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "You see, the blast flung dust and sulfur high into the sky. Some scientists add soot from burning oil. Sunlight dimmed. The planet went cold.",
   "from": 428,
   "to": 658
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Plants died. Then the plant eaters. Then the meat eaters.",
   "from": 661,
   "to": 762
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "About three quarters of all species vanished.",
   "from": 766,
   "to": 822
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "In two thousand seventeen, two Japanese scientists mapped that oily rock worldwide.",
   "from": 826,
   "to": 940
  },
  {
   "i": 8,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "They found it covers only about thirteen percent of Earth's surface.",
   "from": 944,
   "to": 1101
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Anywhere in the other eighty-seven percent, their model says, the cooling is far milder.",
   "from": 1104,
   "to": 1211
  },
  {
   "i": 10,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "The giant dinosaurs might have made it through.",
   "from": 1215,
   "to": 1262
  },
  {
   "i": 11,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Other scientists say the dust and sulfur alone were enough. Nobody can rerun the test.",
   "from": 1265,
   "to": 1371
  },
  {
   "i": 12,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Our ancestors? Small furry animals, most no bigger than a rat.",
   "from": 1374,
   "to": 1479
  },
  {
   "i": 13,
   "speaker": "mammal",
   "text": "Is it safe yet?",
   "from": 1483,
   "to": 1512
  },
  {
   "i": 14,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "With the giants gone, mammals got the planet. They grew. They spread.",
   "from": 1516,
   "to": 1621
  },
  {
   "i": 15,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Today, one of their descendants is watching this video.",
   "from": 1625,
   "to": 1684
  },
  {
   "i": 16,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Unlucky spot for the dinosaurs. Lucky for you.",
   "from": 1688,
   "to": 1772
  }
 ],
